{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>I see him, but not now.</b> It seems to me that this prophecy concerns David, 'but not now' — for it was four hundred years later." "<b>I behold him, but not near.</b> The meaning is doubled, and the metaphor is 'star' and 'scepter.'" "<b>steps forth as a star.</b> There are figures of stars that course through the firmament that were not in the natural order and were not known." "<b>and a scepter shall arise.</b> A kingdom. And it seems correct to me that the word דרך [steps forth] means that it appears in its course like the meaning of the stars in their courses [ממסילותם]. And many interpreted it concerning the Messiah, and the ancients said Sennacherib, who confounded the nations. But behold, Moab and Amalek and Assyria — and more: why did he complete and answer 'beyond'? Only it was fitting for 'steps forth as a star' to be at the end of his metaphor. And those lacking understanding will suppose that one who interprets 'steps forth as a star' of David denies the coming of the Messiah — far be it, far be it. For the Messiah is clearly explained in the prophecy of Daniel, as I interpreted, for he mentioned the standing of the kings of Javan and the uprising of the Hasmoneans and the standing of the Second Temple and the two sieges and the exiles and the salvation, one after another. And there is no need for a prophet in the world alongside the words of Moses, which are the essence: 'If your outcast is at the end of the heavens… and HaShem your Elohim will restore your fortunes.'" "<b>and shatter the corners of Moab.</b> So David did." "<b>and וקרקר.</b> Like מקרקר קיר, and the meaning is 'he destroys the wall,' like מסעף." "<b>children of Seth.</b> Like 'children of Adam,' for he is the foundation [עיקר], for Noah was of his grandchildren. And the Isaaci [Ibn Janah] said that they interpreted it concerning the children of Ammon — also Moab — and the meaning is doubled. Also he said that it is from the same root as וחשפי שת, an allusion to the daughters of Lot. And it seems far to me, for שת is the back [bottom], which is like a foundation [יסוד], as in 'for the foundations [השתות] shall be ruined.' And many interpreted 'all children of Seth' as from the root of השתות, meaning that he shall destroy the provinces — and this is not far."], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:19:28.412354542Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}